THE PANTRY
The pantry is the list of ingredients a recipe app links its recipe lines to. It should cover what is needed to cook most dishes, without bending recipes to fit it. It has two tiers.

CORE: an ingredient a home cook buys in an ordinary supermarket that is used in many dishes, or is needed for a common cuisine (Nordic, Italian, French, Mexican, Indian, Thai, Chinese, Japanese, Middle Eastern, American, baking).
- One entry per thing a shopping list must tell apart: fresh ginger and ground ginger are two; three kinds of cherry tomato are one.
- Cuts at the level recipes actually use: "beef sirloin" is core, "reindeer leg roast" is not.
- Drinks only when food is cooked with them: wine, beer and coconut milk are core candidates, soft drinks are not.

LONG TAIL: a real cooking ingredient that is too specific or too rare for the core. It is kept, because a recipe may need it.

NOT A COOKING INGREDIENT: something you serve or drink rather than cook with (soft drinks, drinking juices, most spirits), or not food at all. It is set inactive, not deleted, so it can return later.

DUPLICATE: the same thing as another pantry entry under a different name ("pita" and "pita bread"). Name the entry it duplicates.

BASE INGREDIENT: for a variant, the plainer pantry entry it belongs to ("thai basil" -> "basil", "smoked paprika" -> "ground paprika"). Leave it empty when there is none.
